Title:
Offensive PowerShell for Red Teamer with Defense Evasion Techniques

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- Long-form red team tradecraft write-up on using PowerShell across the full intrusion lifecycle, with emphasis on defense evasion in modern Windows environments.  
- Covers initial access and payload delivery (download cradles, HTA/VBA/LNK weaponization, and ClickFix/Fake CAPTCHA social engineering), then moves into post-exploitation in AD networks (recon, lateral movement, credential access, and privilege escalation).  
- Details common PowerShell defensive controls (Execution Policy, AMSI, logging/Script Block Logging, ETW, CLM) and practical bypass patterns including obfuscation “string kung-fu,” reflection-based toggles, and in-memory patching/hooking.  
- Includes in-memory .NET assembly execution (reflective loading) to run tools like SharpHound/Seatbelt without dropping binaries, plus examples of LSASS dumping approaches and script/tool customization to reduce detections.  
- Finishes with logging evasion and “covering tracks” techniques (history manipulation, RunMRU tampering, ETW/ScriptBlock bypasses, and event log poisoning).  
- Useful primarily for red teamers/adversary emulation and defenders looking to understand attacker PowerShell TTPs and telemetry blind spots.

Technical Focus:
- AMSI bypass techniques (downgrade, obfuscation, reflection flags, memory patching of AmsiScanBuffer)
- PowerShell download cradles and fileless execution (IEX + IWR/WebClient)
- Weaponization & initial access (HTA, VBA macros, LNK backdooring, ClickFix/Fake CAPTCHA, BitB)
- AD reconnaissance & post-exploitation (PowerView, BloodHound/SharpHound stealth collection)
- In-memory .NET assembly loading (Assembly.Load, gzip/base64 staging)
- Logging/telemetry evasion (Script Block Logging smuggling, ETW patching, .NET Profiler/Invisi-Shell, RunMRU manipulation)

Use Cases:
- Build fileless PowerShell stagers and reverse shells with reduced signature exposure
- Design social-engineering delivery chains (ClickFix/FileFix) for initial access simulations
- Execute .NET offensive tooling in-memory to avoid on-disk detections
- Perform AD enumeration and lateral movement using native remoting/WMI with lower noise
- Test and validate blue-team detections for AMSI/ETW/ScriptBlock logging bypass attempts
- Train incident responders on artifacts like RunMRU, PowerShell history, and event log tampering

Keywords:
PowerShell, AMSI, AmsiScanBuffer, ExecutionPolicy bypass, Invoke-Expression, Invoke-WebRequest, System.Net.WebClient, obfuscation, Invoke-Obfuscation, reflection, VirtualProtect, LoadLibrary, GetProcAddress, ETW, EtwEventWrite, Script Block Logging, AST smuggling, RunMRU, ClickFix, Fake CAPTCHA, Browser-in-the-Browser, HTA, mshta, VBA macro, LNK backdoor, regsvr32, WMI, Win32_Process.Create, PowerView, PowerSploit, BloodHound, SharpHound, in-memory assembly load, Seatbelt, LSASS dump, MiniDumpWriteDump, Invisi-Shell, COR_ENABLE_PROFILING, ConfuserEx